#chapter("Generic Descriptors", [Twenty-four setting-neutral adjectives open the sentence: "I am a [Descriptor]..." Six belong to each pool. Each one gives +2 to that pool, two double-sided tags and a suggested Instinct. Setting books add Descriptors written for their worlds.], epigraph: [The adjective of the character sentence.])

== 1. Descriptor Rules <desc-descriptor-rules>
Each Descriptor gives (Core Rules @core-sentence):

#figure(
  [#table(
    columns: (35%, 65%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Element], [Detail],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Pool bonus];], [+2 to one pool],
    [#strong[Tags];], [2 fixed double-sided tags (each can supply Power or be compelled for Tychē Points)],
    [#strong[Suggested Instinct];], [Use it or write your own],
    [#strong[Identity];], [One line linking the two tags; no mechanical effect],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Each tag has two sides.] Each tag describes something true about your character that can be useful or troublesome. When it helps your action, use it as a power tag under the normal tag rules. When that same trait creates a meaningful complication, it can be compelled under the normal compel rules (Core Rules @core-tags, @core-tyche-points). Neither tag is assigned a helpful or troublesome role: both can do either. The strength and complication must follow naturally from the same phrase. The identity line grants no extra modifier or reward.

#emph[Example:] #emph[Built Like a Door] can supply Power when you force a door or brace against a blow. It can be compelled when your bulk prevents a discreet approach or passage through a cramped space. #emph[Let Me Carry That] helps you haul burdens or carry someone to safety, but can be compelled when you take on a load you should have shared or left behind.

Each entry also has a #strong[hook];: one question to answer at character creation. The answer often becomes your first Belief or a relationship.

Every Descriptor works in any genre. Choose one from this chapter or, when playing in a setting, from that setting book's Descriptors.

